cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or data, please cite it as below."
title: "Roman Urdu SOAP Notes Benchmark: small local LLMs drafting physiotherapy notes from Roman Urdu and English consultations"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-11-15
type: software
abstract: "Small language models that run on a clinic's own computer are attractive for drafting clinical notes, because patient speech never leaves the building. In Pakistan and in UK clinics serving Pakistani communities, many consultations are held in Roman Urdu: Urdu written in Latin script and mixed with English clinical terms. We ask whether two small local models, Qwen2.5 3B and Llama 3.1 8B, write English SOAP notes from Roman Urdu consultations as safely as from English ones. We wrote 20 fictional physiotherapy consultations, each in English and Roman Urdu with identical facts, listed 168 facts (68 critical) and checked every note automatically for omissions, wrong body regions and the injured side. From Roman Urdu, Llama 3.1 8B omitted 16 of 168 facts against 1 from English (exact McNemar p=0.0003) and Qwen2.5 3B omitted 50 against 6 (p<0.0001). Critical facts were lost only in Roman Urdu (8 and 26). The injured side was missing from 13 and 14 of the 14 Roman Urdu notes where a side applied, and reading the notes showed a knee replacement rewritten as a hip replacement, a hip as the lower back, and left as right. In a four-case pilot, translating into English before drafting did not help. A simple rule-based gate flagged 17 of 20 Llama 3.1 8B Roman Urdu notes and none of its English notes. A blinded review of 50 notes by a panel of five physiotherapists agreed with the automatic side check (Cohen's kappa=0.88) and body-region check (kappa=0.75) and with the gate on 90% of notes, but not with the keyword check for critical facts (kappa=0.12, 17 false alarms); the panel also found invented or wrong details in 35 of the 40 Roman Urdu notes. All consultations, notes and code are public."
